{
  "description": "Match request shapes to what Resend accepts. Audiences became Segments and the spec marks `audience_id` deprecated; it is removed from createBroadcast, updateBroadcast, createContact and createSegment, where `segment_id` replaces it. updateContactTopics takes the topic list as the bare JSON body, as resend-node sends it (the spec's `{ topics: [...] }` is refused with 422). updateEmail takes `scheduled_at` in the body; the spec declares no body, so the SDK could not reschedule. createEmailBatch takes the `x-batch-validation` header (`strict`, the default, or `permissive`).\n\nContact properties can be booleans. The spec lists only `string` and `number` for createContactProperty's `type` and types `fallback_value` as a string or number, but Resend accepts `boolean`: a request with `type: \"date\"` was refused with 422 \"The `type` field must be one of \\\"string\\\", \\\"number\\\", or \\\"boolean\\\".\" (live, 2026-10-29). A fallback value must match the property's type, so `fallback_value` also takes a boolean on createContactProperty and updateContactProperty and comes back as one from getContactProperty and listContactProperties.\n\nA custom event's `schema` is a flat map from payload key to type, one of `string`, `number`, `boolean` or `date`. The spec says so in prose but types `schema` as an untyped document on createEvent, updateEvent, getEvent and listEvents. resend-node (`src/events/interfaces/event.ts`, `EventSchemaMap = Record<string, EventSchemaType>`) and Resend's custom events docs give the same four types. All four now target EventSchemaMap; nullability is unchanged.\n\nAn email's custom `headers` are a map from header name to string value. The spec types `headers` as an untyped document on createEmail and on each createEmailBatch item. resend-node (`src/emails/interfaces/create-email-options.interface.ts`) sends `headers?: Record<string, string>`, as the inbox send operations in this spec already type theirs. Both now target EmailHeadersMap.\n\nAn automation step's `config` depends on the step's `type`, which the spec describes only in prose and types as an untyped document. createAutomation and updateAutomation now take each step as a union of eight step shapes, one per `type` (`trigger`, `delay`, `send_email`, `wait_for_event`, `condition`, `contact_update`, `contact_delete`, `add_to_segment`), each with its own config shape, taken from resend-node (`src/automations/interfaces/automation-step.interface.ts`, and the wire names from `src/common/utils/parse-automation-to-api-options.ts`, which maps `eventName` to `event_name`, `replyTo` to `reply_to`, `filterRule` to `filter_rule`, `firstName`/`lastName` to `first_name`/`last_name` and `segmentId` to `segment_id`). A condition rule is one shape for every operator, where resend-node splits rules by operator; the operator's JSDoc says which value each takes. Responses keep AutomationStepResponse, whose `config` stays an untyped document: resend-node types it as `Record<string, unknown>`.",
